🟢 1. Kubernetes Fundamentals — MUST LEARN
# Kubernetes Introduction
Kubernetes ek Container Orchestration Platform hai jo containers ko automatically manage karta hai.

> Simple language me:
Docker ek container banata aur chalata hai. Kubernetes bahut saare Docker containers ko manage karta hai.

Example: Agar tumhari website ke 1000 users aa jaye, to Kubernetes automatically aur containers start kar sakta hai.
> Real-life Example
Socho ek restaurant hai.
Docker = Ek chef jo ek dish banata hai.
Kubernetes = Restaurant manager jo decide karta hai kitne chef chahiye, kaun kaam karega, aur agar koi chef chala jaye to naya chef la deta hai.

> Why Kubernetes?
Agar sirf Docker use karo:
Container crash ho sakta hai.
Traffic badhne par manually naye containers chalane padenge.
Multiple servers par manage karna difficult hai.

Kubernetes ye sab automatically karta hai.
> Kubernetes ki zarurat kyun padi?
Sirf Docker use karne par problems:
Bahut saare containers ko manually manage karna padta hai.
Container crash ho gaya to khud restart karna padega.
Traffic badhne par manually aur containers banana padta hai.
Multiple servers par containers chalana difficult hai.
> kya karta hai
deployment-contain launch karta hai
scaling-trafic badhe to aur container bana deta h
self-Healing-crash container ko restart karta h
load balancing- traffic ko distribute karta h
Rolling updates-app update bina downtime ke
Rollback - problem ho to purana version wapas.


# Why Kubernetes?
Kubernetes is an open-source container orchestration platform that automates the deployment, scaling, networking, and management of containerized applications.

> Why do we use Kubernetes?
Automates application deployment.
Scales applications based on traffic.
Restarts failed containers automatically (Self-Healing).
Distributes traffic using Load Balancing.
Performs Rolling Updates with minimal downtime.
Supports Rollback if a deployment fails.

Manages applications across multiple servers.

One-line interview answer: Kubernetes is used to efficiently manage Docker containers in production by providing automation, scalability, and high availability.


# Kubernetes 
Kubernetes (K8s) is an open-source container orchestration tool originally developed by Google and now maintained by the Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF).

> Main Components
Control Plane: Manages the cluster.
Worker Node: Runs applications.
Pod: Smallest deployable unit containing one or more containers.
Service: Provides network access to Pods.
Deployment: Manages Pod creation, scaling, and updates.

> Simple Flow
Developer → kubectl → API Server → Scheduler → Worker Node → Pod → Container

# Monolithic vs Microservices
Monolithic: A single application where all features are built and deployed together.

Microservices: An architecture where an application is divided into independent services that can be developed, deployed, and scaled separately.

> Monolithic
> Microservices
Single large application
Multiple small independent services
One codebase
Separate codebase for each service
Single deployment
Independent deployment
Scaling the entire application
Scale only the required service
One technology stack
Different technologies can be use
Failure can affect the whole app
Failure is isolated to one service
> Example
Monolithic: One application contains Login, Payment, Orders, and Profile together.
Microservices: Login, Payment, Orders, and Profile run as separate services and communicate through APIs.

# Kubernetes Architecture
Kubernetes Architecture consists of Control Plane (Master Node) and Worker Nodes that work together to run containerized applications.
> Working Flow
Developer runs kubectl apply.
API Server receives the request.
Scheduler selects a Worker Node.
Kubelet creates the Pod.
Container Runtime starts the container.
Kube Proxy routes traffic to the Pod.
> Architecture Summary
Control Plane: Manages the cluster.
Worker Nodes: Run applications.
Pods: Run containers.
API Server: Accepts requests.
Scheduler: Chooses where Pods run.
etcd: Stores cluster data.
Kubelet: Manages Pods.
Kube Proxy: Handles networking.


# Control Plane Components
The Control Plane is the brain of the cluster. It manages the entire Kubernetes cluster.
> Component
> Purpose
-API Server
Entry point for all Kubernetes requests.
-etcd
Stores cluster configuration and state.
-Scheduler
Assigns Pods to the best Worker Node.
-Controller Manager
Ensures the desired state is maintained (restarts failed Pods, creates replicas).


# Worker Node Components
A Worker Node is the machine where Kubernetes actually runs your application inside Pods.
> Components of a Worker Node
> Component
> Purpose
Kubelet
Communicates with the Control Plane and ensures Pods are running correctly.
Container Runtime
Pulls images and runs containers (containerd, CRI-O, Docker).
Kube Proxy
Manages networking and forwards traffic to the correct Pods.
Pods
The smallest deployable unit that contains one or more containers.

> How They Work Together
The Control Plane tells the Worker Node to run a Pod.
Kubelet receives the instruction.
The Container Runtime downloads the image and starts the container.
Kube Proxy routes network traffic to the Pod.

# kubectl — 
kubectl is the command-line tool used to communicate with the Kubernetes cluster. It sends requests to the API Server to create, update, delete, and manage Kubernetes resources.
> Common kubectl Command:
Command
Purpose
kubectl get pods      -List all Pods
kubectl get nodes     -List Worker Nodes
kubectl get services
List Services
kubectl describe pod <name>
View Pod details
kubectl logs <pod-name>
View Pod logs
kubectl apply -f file.yaml
Create or update resources
kubectl delete -f file.yaml
Delete resources
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- bash
Enter a Pod

# Kubernetes YAML
A YAML file is a configuration file used to define Kubernetes resources like Pods, Deployments, and Services.
> Why YAML?
Infrastructure as Code (IaC)
Easy to version with Git
Repeatable deployments

# Cluster, Node & Pod 
1. Cluster
A Cluster is the complete Kubernetes environment consisting of one Control Plane and one or more Worker Nodes.
Example: One company runs 5 servers managed as one Kubernetes Cluster.

2. Node
A Node is a physical or virtual machine inside the cluster where applications run.
Types:
Control Plane Node – Manages the cluster.

Worker Node – Runs Pods.

3. Pod
A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. It contains one or more containers that share the same network and storage.

>Relationship
A Cluster contains multiple Nodes.
Each Node runs multiple Pods.
Each Pod contains one or more Containers.

